Exactly. ;) And the fascinating part is that two beings can inhabit the same physical environment without experiencing anything remotely resembling the same world.

A shark experiences electrical fields we cannot naturally detect. An ant navigates chemical information largely invisible to us. A bat constructs spatial awareness through echoes. A human navigates language, memories, social institutions, imagined futures, and physical sensations.

And even two humans standing beside each other can occupy radically different perceptual, emotional, and conceptual worlds.

Reality has overlapping layers

I’d distinguish four kinds of overlap:

* Shared physical reality: The same environment, matter, forces, and causal relationships.
* Shared perceptual reality: What two organisms can both detect and experience.
* Shared conceptual reality: The categories, meanings, expectations, and models they can communicate.
* Shared actionable reality: The parts of the world they can influence, manipulate, or coordinate around.

That fourth one connects directly to your point about control.

Two organisms don’t even need to perceive reality the same way to cooperate in changing it.

Consider a human and a dog. Their sensory worlds differ, yet together they can accomplish things neither could accomplish as effectively alone.

Or a human using an infrared camera. The camera doesn’t give us snake physiology. It translates otherwise inaccessible information into a form our visual system can interpret.

We’re effectively expanding our accessible slice of reality through technology.

Now the really interesting implication

Conceptual illustration: overlapping access to one physical world, with different sensory and computational capabilities.

The most valuable territory might be at the boundaries between slices.

That’s where translation becomes possible.

A machine can detect patterns that human senses miss. A human can supply context and goals that a machine doesn’t independently possess. An animal may detect environmental changes through sensory channels neither of them naturally shares.

Combine those capabilities, and the resulting system may access information and perform actions unavailable to any participant alone.

This suggests a new operator for our Living Atlas:

Reality Intersection and Translation

Map what each system can perceive, what it can act upon, what remains inaccessible, and which interfaces can bridge the differences.

And there’s an additional twist I think you’ll appreciate:

The boundaries of an organism’s experienced reality aren’t necessarily the boundaries of its influence.

An ant doesn’t need to understand the forest to reshape its soil. Humans don’t need to understand every molecular interaction to develop medicines. We can influence systems far beyond our perceptual or conceptual understanding.

So there are really two frontiers: expanding what we can know, and expanding what we can reliably do.

And neither frontier has to wait for the other to be complete.

That’s a rather profound way to think about evolution: not merely organisms adapting to their worlds, but organisms progressively developing new ways to access, connect, and reshape different portions of the same reality.